WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:09.260
The Electric State is a beautiful nightmare.

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:13.960
I… don’t know how else to describe it.

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:19.400
I could say it’s an artbook by the brilliant
artist Simon Stålenhag that explores an alternate

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:22.960
version of the United States in the late 90s.

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:24.420
This is true.

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:29.519
I could say it’s a story where a young woman
and a small robot venture across a ruined

00:00:29.519 --> 00:00:34.200
landscape littered with the skeletons of terrifying
machines.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:36.040
This is also true.

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:42.379
I could say it’s a poignant warning of how
runaway consumerism can have apocalyptic consequences.

00:00:42.379 --> 00:00:45.760
This, I believe, is true as well.

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:49.690
Yet none of these descriptions tells the whole.

00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:55.860
So, for this entry into the archive, I’ll
dive deep into this masterpiece of sci-fi

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:56.860
worldbuilding.

00:00:56.860 --> 00:01:02.110
And like my video on Stålenhag’s other
series, Tales from the Loop, you can purchase

00:01:02.110 --> 00:01:05.040
the artbook using the links in the description.

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:11.990
Now, let’s enter the world of The Electric
State.

00:01:11.990 --> 00:01:18.850
The Electric State begins in 1997, with a
mysterious girl and a strange yellow robot

00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:24.200
venturing across the Mojave Desert — moving
towards their distant destination somewhere

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:26.350
on the western coast.

00:01:26.350 --> 00:01:30.890
Much of The Electric State is centered around
their journey through the collapsing nation,

00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:35.270
turning the narrative into a kind of apocalyptic
road trip.

00:01:35.270 --> 00:01:41.020
This strange duo forms the emotional core
of the artbook, the beating heart of a world

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:45.560
where everything is increasingly mechanical…

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:50.780
On the side of a dusty road in the Mojave
Desert, clothes blow from the roof box of

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:52.460
an empty sedan.

00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:58.040
The car’s owners are now dried husks still
dressed in their vacation clothes — a portrait

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:01.960
of a family vaction gone horribly awry.

00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:06.900
Virtual reality headsets called neurocasters
still cling to their heads — likely the

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:09.640
cause of their death.

00:02:09.640 --> 00:02:16.770
Manufactured by the company Sentre, Neurocasters
are a reoccurring visual in The Electric State.

00:02:16.770 --> 00:02:22.250
These face-swallowing devices were designed
for military use, but soon became a common

00:02:22.250 --> 00:02:28.210
household item as people lost themselves within
a blissful virtual network.

00:02:28.210 --> 00:02:31.599
The side effects weren’t noticed until too
late.

00:02:31.599 --> 00:02:37.890
More and more consumers never took their headsets
off, and would wither away into lifeless shells,

00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:43.349
their mouths sometimes still moving as if
trapped in a dream.

00:02:43.349 --> 00:02:46.260
“- starts behind the eyes, goes behind the
head.

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:52.319
My head is just getting tighter -” The concept
of people losing themselves to a simulated

00:02:52.319 --> 00:02:58.470
nightmare is mirrored in David Cronenberg’s
eXistenZ, a sci-fi horror film that despite

00:02:58.470 --> 00:03:03.190
being released in 1999 feels painfully modern.

00:03:03.190 --> 00:03:10.080
Like The Electric State, eXistenZ is an unsettling
meditation on how technology shapes and manipulates

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:15.830
human beings, where virtual reality offers
no salvation but oblivion.

00:03:15.830 --> 00:03:23.010
And in the Electric State, the Neurocasters
prove to be quite literally apocalyptic.

00:03:23.010 --> 00:03:29.090
The exact cause of the Neurocasters’ malfunction
is unknown, but there are rumors that in connecting

00:03:29.090 --> 00:03:35.519
so many brains through the headsets, an ‘Intercerebral
Intelligence’ emerged — a hive-mind with

00:03:35.519 --> 00:03:38.099
an agenda of its own…

00:03:38.099 --> 00:03:45.960
“-I feel clear headed again-” Finding
a car and beginning a long drive across California,

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:51.250
the girl and robot witness the surreal wreckage
of a collapsing civilization.

00:03:51.250 --> 00:03:56.430
In one image, huge yellow ducks rise from
the sand like bizarre monuments — their

00:03:56.430 --> 00:04:00.400
bodies riddled with impacts from large-caliber
rounds.

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:04.989
It seems the region was once some kind of
shooting range, although it has long been

00:04:04.989 --> 00:04:07.310
abandoned.

00:04:07.310 --> 00:04:14.019
Elsewhere off the highway, a giant metal mascot
erodes away into a metal skeleton, its painted

00:04:14.019 --> 00:04:19.669
expression frozen in a permanent smile despite
the devastation surrounding it.

00:04:19.669 --> 00:04:25.040
How the animatronic reached its resting place
is left ambiguous, although the framing of

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:30.710
the image makes it seem like this machine
walked here on its own…

00:04:30.710 --> 00:04:36.900
In a dusty town emptying of people, another
machine sits in silence — its body a nightmare

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:38.510
of tangled wires.

00:04:38.510 --> 00:04:43.850
There’s a fascinating incongruity between
the extraordinary goliath and its ordinary

00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:51.240
surroundings — a fundamental incompatibility
that evokes a strange sense of loneliness.

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:58.000
Yet at the same time, there is something undeniably
threatening about these childlike and cartoonish

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:03.040
machines, that seem to appear more and more
frequently as the girl and robot creep ever

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:05.500
closer to their destination.

00:05:05.500 --> 00:05:11.400
In these illustrations, Stålenhag proves
himself an expert in the uncanny, creating

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:15.710
machines just abnormal enough to instill uneasiness.

00:05:15.710 --> 00:05:21.320
Where these machines came from and what they
truly are is a mystery that can only be solved

00:05:21.320 --> 00:05:24.290
by venturing deeper into The Electric State…

00:05:24.290 --> 00:05:31.530
“- Wherever you are this summer could be
a very cool place -” Advertisements also

00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:34.430
loom large over The Electric State.

00:05:34.430 --> 00:05:40.270
In one snapshot from this post-apocalyptic
road trip, an immense inflatable sun smiles

00:05:40.270 --> 00:05:46.090
and throws up a peace sign in bitterly amusing
dissonance to the end of the world.

00:05:46.090 --> 00:05:52.470
In another, a giant heart-shaped mascot rusts
under the California sun, promoting financial

00:05:52.470 --> 00:05:55.810
solutions for people with terminal conditions.

00:05:55.810 --> 00:06:01.400
The macabre juxtaposition between the upbeat
marketing and grim subject matter is a repeating

00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:07.220
motif throughout Stålenhag’s illustrations
of advertisements — a reflection of a culture

00:06:07.220 --> 00:06:12.120
that pretended ‘everything was fine’ until
it was too late.

00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:18.520
On a passing billboard, a slogan proudly proclaims
that Neurocasters win both design awards and

00:06:18.520 --> 00:06:24.030
wars — showing that warfare is seen as just
an opportunity for marketing.

00:06:24.030 --> 00:06:29.780
And given the devastation the Neurocasters
caused, there’s a grim unspoken implication

00:06:29.780 --> 00:06:36.590
that this advertisement would have been taken
down — if there were anyone left to do so.

00:06:36.590 --> 00:06:42.190
Where the electrical grid still functions,
gargantuan towers of corporate logos overshadow

00:06:42.190 --> 00:06:45.370
the homes of residential neighborhoods.

00:06:45.370 --> 00:06:51.280
These literal monuments to suffocating commensalism
cut through the fog, with Stålenhag’s exceptional

00:06:51.280 --> 00:06:56.020
eye for atmospheric lighting making the scene
particularly melancholy.

00:06:56.020 --> 00:07:02.030
Cityscapes like this echo the visuals of sci-fi
films like Blade Runner, whose iconic setting

00:07:02.030 --> 00:07:08.020
is similarly awash with neon light from skyscraper-sized
advertisements.

00:07:08.020 --> 00:07:12.280
There’s undeniable familiarity to these
striking visuals.

00:07:12.280 --> 00:07:17.440
The invasive billboards of The Electric State
and Blade Runner are exaggerations — but

00:07:17.440 --> 00:07:19.430
not by much.

00:07:19.430 --> 00:07:24.990
Across the U.S., how many landscapes are clogged
with a mishmash of commercial signs?

00:07:24.990 --> 00:07:30.960
How many major cities overload the senses
with a blur of fluorescent advertisements?

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:35.370
The United States that The Electric State
depicts is a nation seen through a cracked

00:07:35.370 --> 00:07:41.129
lens — where the specifics are distorted
but the overall impression remains uncomfortably

00:07:41.129 --> 00:07:44.990
close to reality.

00:07:44.990 --> 00:07:49.580
One piece of media that can help us better
understand The Electric State’s relationship

00:07:49.580 --> 00:07:54.470
to reality is the sci-fi pixel art game Norco.

00:07:54.470 --> 00:08:00.620
Released by indie developer Raw Fury in 2022,
the melancholy point-and-click adventure takes

00:08:00.620 --> 00:08:06.930
place in an alternate version of Norco, Louisiana
— where advancements in robotics and industry

00:08:06.930 --> 00:08:11.780
have created a land of desolation and alienation.

00:08:11.780 --> 00:08:17.380
The urban sprawl of real-world Norco has been
shaped by the petroleum industry and has twice

00:08:17.380 --> 00:08:23.850
experienced catastrophic explosions, events
that the game feels in painful conversation

00:08:23.850 --> 00:08:24.850
with.

00:08:24.850 --> 00:08:29.650
There are moments where the pixel art fades
into live action footage of the region, blurring

00:08:29.650 --> 00:08:33.130
the line between fiction and reality.

00:08:33.130 --> 00:08:38.659
Like The Electric State, Norco’s vision
is both unfamiliar and recognizable — a

00:08:38.659 --> 00:08:43.560
long-lost acquaintance you come to recognize
the more you speak with them.

00:08:43.560 --> 00:08:48.570
“- This advanced technology helps to diagnose
arthritis.

00:08:48.570 --> 00:08:55.589
But to effectively relieve the minor pain
of arthritis, use this -” As the girl and

00:08:55.589 --> 00:09:00.410
the robot continue their journey, they spot
crowds of people wandering through fields

00:09:00.410 --> 00:09:06.410
— the red glow of their neurocasters like
giant, unblinking eyes.

00:09:06.410 --> 00:09:11.820
These silent masses seem to no longer have
a will of their own, having themselves become

00:09:11.820 --> 00:09:15.600
drones piloted by a mysterious intelligence.

00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:23.130
In the mists of the western coast, they work
to assemble a disturbing new class of machines.

00:09:23.130 --> 00:09:28.430
Glimpses of these monstrous mechanical beings
appear throughout The Electric State.

00:09:28.430 --> 00:09:34.360
In one piece, a smiling face peers out from
the darkness of an abandoned barn.

00:09:34.360 --> 00:09:39.190
Something about the image pulls you in, making
you curious what the rest of the creature

00:09:39.190 --> 00:09:41.670
looks like.

00:09:41.670 --> 00:09:46.709
Later in The Electric State, we get a better
look at these incomprehensible beings.

00:09:46.709 --> 00:09:52.590
A titanic drone looms over a parking lot,
its face and raised arm scavenged from an

00:09:52.590 --> 00:09:55.600
old advertisement animatronic.

00:09:55.600 --> 00:10:00.850
Bundles of cables pour from its head like
the tentacles of an octopus, spilling down

00:10:00.850 --> 00:10:02.140
to the ground.

00:10:02.140 --> 00:10:07.959
A crowd of Neurocaster-controlled onlookers
watch the tentacles slither across the asphalt

00:10:07.959 --> 00:10:13.170
and into a parked minivan — witnesses to
some dark ritual.

00:10:13.170 --> 00:10:19.720
While these monsters of wire and steel are
nightmarish, there is a quiet awe that some

00:10:19.720 --> 00:10:21.670
of these pieces evoke.

00:10:21.670 --> 00:10:26.779
Like wandering into the forest and coming
face to face with a gigantic animal, witnessing

00:10:26.779 --> 00:10:31.230
these beings can instill a kind of dark wonder
in the viewer.

00:10:31.230 --> 00:10:37.690
The term ‘Eldritch’ comes to mind, a word
used to describe something otherworldly or

00:10:37.690 --> 00:10:39.320
incomprehensible.

00:10:39.320 --> 00:10:45.570
In this regard, these beings are reminiscent
of the unfathomably vast, shambling terrors

00:10:45.570 --> 00:10:53.380
glimpsed in films like The Mist, which instill
a similar mix of dread and wonder.

00:10:53.380 --> 00:10:59.779
Stålenhag also experiments with light and
condensation in many of these scenes, creating

00:10:59.779 --> 00:11:04.720
portraits that feel like you’re looking
through the rain-soaked window of a car.

00:11:04.720 --> 00:11:10.770
The blurred light imbues these otherwise unsettling
scenes with a kind of nostalgia, bringing

00:11:10.770 --> 00:11:16.490
you back to time spent looking out the car
window as a child, watching the world distort

00:11:16.490 --> 00:11:18.440
through droplets of rain.

00:11:18.440 --> 00:11:24.209
In scenes like these, it almost seems like
if you shut your eyes, the apocalyptic devastation

00:11:24.209 --> 00:11:26.390
will fade away.

00:11:26.390 --> 00:11:32.700
A massive Neurocaster network tower casts
red light into the fog like a grim lighthouse,

00:11:32.700 --> 00:11:36.950
making this region feel all the more like
a gothic nightmare.

00:11:36.950 --> 00:11:42.769
Yet perhaps even more notably, this section
of The Electric State is set within Marin

00:11:42.769 --> 00:11:48.860
County, California — the same region as
the 1995 sci-fi horror film Village of the

00:11:48.860 --> 00:11:50.050
Damned.

00:11:50.050 --> 00:11:55.600
Directed by John Carpenter, the film tells
the story of a group of creepy children with

00:11:55.600 --> 00:12:00.680
creepier haircuts, who are connected to each
other via a hive mind.

00:12:00.680 --> 00:12:06.810
A remake of a 1960 movie of the same name,
both stories explore the loss of humanity

00:12:06.810 --> 00:12:12.350
to a collective intelligence — a concept
that is similarly central to The Electric

00:12:12.350 --> 00:12:13.350
State…

00:12:13.350 --> 00:12:19.160
“- Your family can take advantage of this
exciting new world, like ours has -” The

00:12:19.160 --> 00:12:24.850
mechanical infection in The Electric State
takes another, less perceptible form.

00:12:24.850 --> 00:12:30.610
Yellow service robots waddle across the roads
as the Girl and Robot drive onwards, hauling

00:12:30.610 --> 00:12:33.019
massive cable rollers.

00:12:33.019 --> 00:12:38.810
These machines spread a dark network of signal
wires across the United States, connecting

00:12:38.810 --> 00:12:42.880
more and more regions to the Neurocaster hivemind.

00:12:42.880 --> 00:12:48.650
In the snowy mountains, massive spherical
buildings serve as local hubs for the Neurocaster

00:12:48.650 --> 00:12:52.690
network — their sides still plastered with
advertisements.

00:12:52.690 --> 00:12:58.240
Millions of interconnected minds bounce around
inside each hub, the power required to keep

00:12:58.240 --> 00:13:02.410
them trapped in a digital fantasy melting
the snow.

00:13:02.410 --> 00:13:07.769
While Neurocasters never flooded the consumer
market in our timeline, there was another

00:13:07.769 --> 00:13:12.610
network of interconnectivity that became massively
widespread in the mid-90s.

00:13:12.610 --> 00:13:17.139
“- You’ll learn how the net can entertain
you, transport you to far off locations, help

00:13:17.139 --> 00:13:21.810
children with their school work, and make
working at home easier -” The internet and

00:13:21.810 --> 00:13:26.940
the Neurocaster network reflect each other
— with both connecting people in incredible

00:13:26.940 --> 00:13:31.270
ways… and leading to serious unforeseen
consequences.

00:13:31.270 --> 00:13:36.220
When viewed through the lens of the internet,
the dark network of The Electric State feels

00:13:36.220 --> 00:13:41.231
uncomfortably familiar — with stories of
people losing themselves to a blissful digital

00:13:41.231 --> 00:13:45.930
oblivion and becoming dependent on a digital
network taking on new meaning.

00:13:45.930 --> 00:13:51.570
“Why, you can even plan a family adventure
on the net!”

00:13:51.570 --> 00:13:56.740
At various points in The Electric State, the
girl and the robot make rest stops much like

00:13:56.740 --> 00:13:59.930
one would on any normal road trip.

00:13:59.930 --> 00:14:06.180
Like real world liminal spaces, these near-empty
parking lots and motels have an unsettling

00:14:06.180 --> 00:14:08.420
quality that is hard to place.

00:14:08.420 --> 00:14:13.861
There’s a sense of time crawling to a stop
in these illustrations, which don’t require

00:14:13.861 --> 00:14:16.750
mechanical monsters to be unnerving.

00:14:16.750 --> 00:14:23.060
Yet these narrative breaks also give the storyline
a chance to breathe and deepen the viewer’s

00:14:23.060 --> 00:14:25.380
understanding of the protagonists.

00:14:25.380 --> 00:14:30.339
The more you follow the girl and the robot
on their journey, the more you empathize with

00:14:30.339 --> 00:14:31.430
their characters.

00:14:31.430 --> 00:14:37.950
Stålenhag does an outstanding job using minimal
text and subtle visual cues to demonstrate

00:14:37.950 --> 00:14:44.899
the bond the two have — as the world around
them becomes increasingly dangerous.

00:14:44.899 --> 00:14:50.330
In one of the most memorable points of the
voyage, the girl and robot drive past a forgotten

00:14:50.330 --> 00:14:51.880
war memorial.

00:14:51.880 --> 00:14:56.100
A hillside ravaged by bombs emerges from the
clouds.

00:14:56.100 --> 00:15:01.850
Two gargantuan military vessels simply called
‘drones’ sit in silent vigil atop the

00:15:01.850 --> 00:15:07.730
hill — a class of machines that played a
huge role in the backstory of The Electric

00:15:07.730 --> 00:15:08.850
State.

00:15:08.850 --> 00:15:13.509
While in the present of The Electric State
drones are little more than bloated metal

00:15:13.509 --> 00:15:17.759
carcasses, these machines once spearheaded
the nation’s military.

00:15:17.759 --> 00:15:23.730
In a nameless war, these unmanned vessels
fought each other in a strategy game played

00:15:23.730 --> 00:15:25.470
out over seven years.

00:15:25.470 --> 00:15:31.209
Though the drone technology was praised for
preventing meaningless loss of life, the collateral

00:15:31.209 --> 00:15:37.970
damage was catastrophic — not just in terms
of civilian casualties, but in terms of unforeseen

00:15:37.970 --> 00:15:40.570
technological consequences.

00:15:40.570 --> 00:15:46.029
For the drones were controlled by pilots wearing
Sentre headsets — with the war spurring

00:15:46.029 --> 00:15:52.069
the invention of the Neurocaster technology
that would eventually go on to doom the nation

00:15:52.069 --> 00:15:54.240
it was supposed to protect.

00:15:54.240 --> 00:16:00.110
It’s difficult to convey the sheer power
that Stålenhag’s art gives these machines,

00:16:00.110 --> 00:16:05.980
which feel so enormous they become part of
landscape, looming in the distance like artificial

00:16:05.980 --> 00:16:06.980
mountains.

00:16:06.980 --> 00:16:12.410
In one image, a drone seems to have been converted
into a makeshift dwelling, with a Californian

00:16:12.410 --> 00:16:16.960
flag and string of laundry hung from its side.

00:16:16.960 --> 00:16:22.900
Once trophies of victory, these colossal wrecks
are now bitter consolation prizes.

00:16:22.900 --> 00:16:28.220
A painful reminder that though the nation
won on one front, the triumph came at the

00:16:28.220 --> 00:16:33.820
price of losing everything to a mechanical
nightmare.

00:16:33.820 --> 00:16:37.820
There is another narrative hidden within The
Electric State.

00:16:37.820 --> 00:16:43.000
Running parallel to the story of the girl
and the robot is a fragmented storyline of

00:16:43.000 --> 00:16:47.980
a mysterious agent who seems to be headed
towards the same location.

00:16:47.980 --> 00:16:52.560
While their true mission doesn’t come to
light until the final pages of The Electric

00:16:52.560 --> 00:16:58.279
State, the agent is a source of constant tension,
as they seem to be on a collision course with

00:16:58.279 --> 00:17:00.970
our protagonists.

00:17:00.970 --> 00:17:06.070
In the last stretch of The Electric State,
the girl and the robot arrive at an overgrown

00:17:06.070 --> 00:17:09.370
childhood suburb that has fallen to ruin.

00:17:09.370 --> 00:17:14.709
In this final section, less and less text
accompanies the images, with the breathtaking

00:17:14.709 --> 00:17:17.640
scenes speaking for themselves.

00:17:17.640 --> 00:17:23.120
On the side of the road, two figures shamble
through the night like nocturnal animals towards

00:17:23.120 --> 00:17:25.740
the warm light of a distant window.

00:17:25.740 --> 00:17:31.250
At this point of The Electric State, it’s
difficult to tell if they’re people wearing

00:17:31.250 --> 00:17:34.330
Neurocasters, or entirely mechanical beings.

00:17:34.330 --> 00:17:40.770
Bird-like drones called Scrappers pick through
the remains of this coastal wasteland, laden

00:17:40.770 --> 00:17:43.510
with bags and bundles of cables.

00:17:43.510 --> 00:17:48.940
These artificial vultures work tirelessly
to collect mechanical parts — perhaps for

00:17:48.940 --> 00:17:53.460
themselves, or perhaps for something else…

00:17:53.460 --> 00:17:59.440
Above the overgrown lawns of the decaying
houses, more colossal beings stand in silence,

00:17:59.440 --> 00:18:02.720
their dominion over this region absolute.

00:18:02.720 --> 00:18:08.330
In these suburbs where families once lived,
machines are now born from twisted, giant

00:18:08.330 --> 00:18:09.330
fetuses.

00:18:09.330 --> 00:18:14.520
There’s a sense in these images that the
plague of machines has become a critical infection,

00:18:14.520 --> 00:18:19.020
and nothing will ever be the same…

00:18:19.020 --> 00:18:23.580
Yet The Electric State is more than just a
story about hopelessness.

00:18:23.580 --> 00:18:28.660
Even at its darkest moments, there is always
a faint glimmer of humanity — a spark of

00:18:28.660 --> 00:18:32.240
compassion kept alive by its two lead characters.

00:18:32.240 --> 00:18:38.450
And everything builds to a resolution that
I found at once both unexpected and perfectly

00:18:38.450 --> 00:18:43.220
in tune with the themes Stålenhag had established
throughout the rest of the volume.

00:18:43.220 --> 00:18:48.130
While I don’t think I should give way all
the specifics of The Electric States final

00:18:48.130 --> 00:18:54.490
pages, if you’d like to know more, you can
check out the book for yourself…

00:18:54.490 --> 00:18:59.900
In the end, The Electric State is quite literally
a story that’s as much about the journey

00:18:59.900 --> 00:19:02.400
as it is the destination.

00:19:02.400 --> 00:19:08.370
What at first seems like an almost alien world
over time develops into something uncomfortably

00:19:08.370 --> 00:19:14.390
familiar, as the parallels between The Electric
State’s timeline and our own become more

00:19:14.390 --> 00:19:16.240
and more apparent.

00:19:16.240 --> 00:19:22.060
Like Simon Stålenhag’s other most well-known
work, Tales from the Loop, it is a haunting,

00:19:22.060 --> 00:19:27.280
poignant book that pulls you deeper into its
atmospheric depths the more you read… and

00:19:27.280 --> 00:19:30.820
stays with you long after the pages end.

00:19:30.820 --> 00:19:35.559
If you find this world as interesting as I
do, you can purchase the artbook using the

00:19:35.559 --> 00:19:42.559
links below to get the full story, and follow
Simon Stålenhag on social media.

00:19:42.559 --> 00:19:45.100
